  "text": "To solve the given product, we first simplify each fraction in the product. Let's start by recalling the change of base formula for logarithms:\n\\[ \\log_a(b) = \\frac{\\log_c(b)}{\\log_c(a)} \\]\nwhere \\(c\\) is any positive number different from 1.\n\nLet's apply this formula to each fraction in the product:\n\n1. For the first fraction:\n\\[ \\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2-4})} = \\frac{\\frac{\\log_k(5)}{\\log_k(5)} \\cdot (k^2-1)}{\\frac{\\log_{k+1}(5)}{\\log_{k+1}(5)} \\cdot (k^2-4)} = \\frac{(k^2-1)\\log_{k+1}(5)}{(k^2-4)\\log_k(5)} \\]\n\n2. For the second fraction:\n\\[ \\frac{\\log_5(5^{24})}{\\log_6(5^{21})} = \\frac{24\\log_5(5)}{21\\log_6(5)} = \\frac{24}{21\\log_6(5)} \\]\n\n3. For the third fraction:\n\\[ \\frac{\\log_6(5^{35})}{\\log_7(5^{32})} = \\frac{35\\log_6(5)}{32\\log_7(5)} \\]\n\nWe can see a pattern emerging. Each fraction simplifies to a form involving the logarithm of 5 with different bases. To find the product, we multiply all these fractions together:\n\\[ \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k^2-1)\\log_{k+1}(5)}{(k^2-4)\\log_k(5)} \\cdot \\frac{24}{21\\log_6(5)} \\cdot \\frac{35\\log_6(5)}{32\\log_7(5)} \\cdots \\frac{3968\\log_{64}(5)}{3965\\log_{63}(5)} \\]\n\nNotice that the \\(\\log_{k+1}(5)\\) and \\(\\log_k(5)\\) terms cancel out, as do most of the other terms. We are left with:\n\\[ \\frac{24 \\cdot 35 \\cdots 3968}{21 \\cdot 32 \\cdots 3965} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_{64}(5)}{\\log_{63}(5)} \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ \\frac{24 \\cdot 35 \\cdots 3968}{21 \\cdot 32 \\cdots 3965} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_{64}(5)}{\\log_{63}(5)} \\]\n\nThe product of the first terms is the ratio of two factorials:\n\\[ \\frac{24 \\cdot 35 \\cdots 3968}{21 \\cdot 32 \\cdots 3965} = \\frac{3968!}{21! \\cdot 3965!} \\]\n\nThe ratio of the logarithms is:\n\\[ \\frac{\\log_{64}(5)}{\\log_{63}(5)} = \\frac{\\log_5(5)}{\\log_5(64)} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_5(64)}{\\log_5(63)} \\cdots \\frac{\\log_5(63)}{\\log_5(5)} = 1 \\]\n\nSo, the entire product simplifies to:\n\\[ \\frac{3968!}{21! \\cdot 3965!} \\]\n\nSince the factorial in the numerator and denominator cancels out most of the terms, we are left with:\n\\[ \\frac{3968}{21} \\]\n\nThus, \\(m = 3968\\) and \\(n = 21\\), and \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are relatively prime. Therefore, \\(m + n = 3968 + 21 = 3989\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{3989}\\).",
